Sharp Windows CE loader for Brain boards
=======================================

Boots Sharp's Windows CE from the internal MMC, read only, after patching it in
RAM from pwsh2-boot/boot.nkp on the card. Used by pwsh2-boot.

Files
-----

sharpce.c	bootsharpce, board_late_init, boot command
nkpatch.c	boot.nkp bundle: skip spans, patches, module loads
hwstate.c	EBOOT register snapshot at entry, restored before the CE jump
bootlog.c	savelog: console record to pwsh2-boot/uboot.log
keyboard.c	I2C keyboard: console input and brainkey
brain.h		interface shared with board code

Boards
------

Enabled by CONFIG_SHARP_WINCE in a <board>_ce_defconfig. The upstream
defconfigs are left as they are and build upstream U-Boot.

Per-device values are Kconfig options set in the defconfig:

SHARP_WINCE_BSP_ARGS	EBOOT's internal boot flag (SH2 0x400F3060, A7300 0x400F6C60)
SHARP_WINCE_SLOT_B	fall back to the second image slot
SHARP_WINCE_DEBUG_LOG	save CE's RAM debug log to the card
SHARP_WINCE_BOOTCMD	replaces bootcmd; the old one is kept as linux_boot
SHARP_KEYBOARD		I2C keyboard at 0x28
SHARP_KEYBOARD_I2C_BUS	SH2 1, A7300 0

Board code may provide (weak defaults do nothing):

brain_board_late_init()	start a display, keyboard console, pin setup
brain_menu_progress()	progress while loading
brain_display_report()	log display state before the CE jump
brain_display_refresh()	called while waiting for console input

With CONFIG_SHARP_WINCE set, board code must leave EBOOT's clocks and pin mux
as it can: CE expects them. pwsh2 and pwa7200 skip their early clock setup and
set the SSP clocks just before MMC init instead.

Adding a device
---------------

1. Find the BSP arguments flag in the device's EBOOT (see pwsh2-boot docs).
2. Copy the closest <board>_ce_defconfig, set TARGET_* and the values above.
3. Gate any board code that changes clocks or pins with CONFIG_SHARP_WINCE.
4. Put device-only code (display, diagnostics) in the board directory.
